/* ═══ v2.3.5301: THE SWITCH TABLE (grand plan step 7.1, card T PR 1) ═══
 *
 * Every switch of the game, one row a name: every live flag the worker reads
 * (server/src/liveops.js) and every `?name=` the page reads.  The ONE list of
 * names (the plan's rule 2): the report's `sw` (SHAPES part 5) and the wire
 * table's `flag` column are to read it.  The rules: docs/specs/switch-table.md;
 * the page made from it: docs/SWITCHES.md (`node tools/guards/switches.test.mjs
 * --write`, never by hand); the guard: tools/guards/switches.test.mjs.
 *
 * DATA, NO I/O.  Where each name is READ is never typed here: the scanner
 * finds it (tools/guards/lib/switchScan.mjs) and the page prints it.  A row
 * is judged by a person reading the code at the read; the scanner never
 * writes one.
 *
 * THE COLUMNS (every row has every one; the guard fails a row without):
 *   name      as it is thrown: a live flag's name (`farm`), a page switch
 *             with its `?` (`?guest`, from PR 2), a memory saver by its id
 *             (`sfxcache`: `off_sfxcache` on the worker, `?sfxcache=0` on the
 *             page, one row).
 *   side      `worker`, `page` or `both`.
 *   system    card B's (tools/guards/lib/systems.mjs); a name that fits two
 *             takes the system of the file its read is in.
 *   kind      `switch` (a way of the game turned off or on), `value` (a
 *             number or a word to try: `xp_mult`) or `lever` (a test's or the
 *             owner's tool: `testmode`, `?guest=1`).
 *   born      the tag of the change that made it (the number after `v2.3.`),
 *             from the comment at its read or `git log -S`; `'unknown'` when
 *             neither says (counted, never guessed).
 *   on        (a `switch` row's, v2.3.5305) the day its way went on for
 *             everyone, from git: the day the commit of the change that made
 *             it the default reached main's first-parent line (UTC).  For
 *             most rows that change is `born`; for an opt-in made the default
 *             later, `onBy` names the later change.  `'off'`: its way is not
 *             on for everyone yet (an opt-in: `arenasettleplan`).  `'unknown'`
 *             only where `born` is.  The end date's rule reads it
 *             (`switches.test.mjs --due`; docs/specs/switch-table.md).
 *   onBy      (only where `on` is not `born`'s day) the tag that turned it on
 *             for everyone (`?onepic`: born 4885 behind `=1`, on at 4990).
 *   thrown    on the worker the value that throws it (`false` for a lower-case
 *             kill switch, `true` for `disable_*`, `off_*` and a put-away
 *             feature's `true`), or the value's range; on the page its form
 *             (`present`, `=1`, `=0` and the words its read also takes, or a
 *             value).
 *   risk      `logic`, `memory` or `chip` (`none` for a lever): what the old
 *             way guards against.
 *   needs     the names it needs (rule 4: it turns off with them).
 *   fallback  `'no'`, or the trigger that still uses its old way, as names in
 *             a file (`'onWindows, onMac in src/game/deviceProfile.js'`).
 *   reader    (a worker row with needs, v2.3.5304) the function that decides
 *             it, as `fn in file`: the guard asks that it reads each need
 *             (rule 4: a dependent turns off with what it needs only if its
 *             reader asks).
 *   never     (optional) why it may never go off (a ledger): the guard fails
 *             it while a switch it needs is still a row (the switch beneath
 *             is removed first).
 *   off       one sentence: what throwing it turns off, or back on.
 *
 * CAPS.  A cap is a row only where the worker reads it as a flag or a comment
 * promises it as a kill switch (liveops.test §8f, §8g: `storeGear`,
 * `dailyChest`, `whirlWindup`, `broCount` among them).  Any other cap's
 * override is the emergency lever of docs/specs/liveops.md, and stays out.
 *
 * ORDER.  Three sections, by side: the switches the worker throws for
 * the page (`both`), then the worker, then the page; in each, the rows sorted
 * by name (its `?` aside, lower case).  A new row goes where its name puts
 * it, never at the end: two PRs that each add a row then touch the same
 * lines only when their names are neighbours (the gatekeeper's sitting 11:
 * every row-adding PR used to collide at the array's end).  The guard fails
 * a row out of its place (check 10).  A new switch adds its row in its own
 * PR (the guard fails it otherwise).
 */
export const SIDES = Object.freeze(['worker', 'page', 'both']);
export const KINDS = Object.freeze(['switch', 'value', 'lever']);
export const RISKS = Object.freeze(['logic', 'memory', 'chip', 'none']);
export const COLUMNS = Object.freeze(['name', 'side', 'system', 'kind', 'born', 'thrown', 'risk', 'needs', 'fallback', 'off']);
/* v2.3.5304: columns only some rows carry (rule 4, card T PR 4); v2.3.5305:
   `on` (every `switch` row's, and no other's) and `onBy` (the end date) */
export const OPTIONAL = Object.freeze(['reader', 'never', 'thrownFails', 'on', 'onBy']);

/* v2.3.5305: THE FALLBACKS (a census: only shrinks; the gatekeeper's).  A
   fallback is a trigger that still uses a switch's old way (its row's
   `fallback`), kept here apart from the rows so a row deleted cannot take its
   fallback with it.  While its trigger is in its file the old way's read
   stays, and so does the row (the guard's check 12); the entry and the row's
   fallback leave together, in the PR that removes the trigger
   (docs/specs/switch-table.md, the end date). */
export const FALLBACKS = Object.freeze({
  'lookpalette': 'onWindows, onMac (v2.3.5228: the look\'s overrides, drawLanes.js) in src/game/deviceProfile.js',
  '?burstnumbers': 'DecompressionStream in src/rendering/levelUpBurstNumbers.js',
  '?folkskills': 'folkSkillReady in src/rendering/folkSkill.js',
  '?musicdecode': '_deckFailed in src/data/gameDisplay.js',
  '?onebatch': 'softRenderer, onWindows, onMac (v2.3.5228: the program\'s overrides, drawLanes.js) in src/game/deviceProfile.js',
});
